06d784f21167c65a.statement:
ROBUST BALANCED CORRELATED-ENERGY COROLLARY.

Fix an integer (Jge1), a real constant (Cge1), and real scales (Q,H,S,Tge1). For each integer (q) with (Qle q<2Q), choose a unit (A_q) modulo (q). Let (
u_h,a_s,b_t) be complex coefficients supported respectively on
[
 Hle h<2H,qquad Sle s<2S,qquad Tle t<2T,
]
and satisfying
[
 |
u_h|le	au_J(h),qquad |a_s|le	au_J(s),qquad |b_t|le	au_J(t).
]
Define
[
 W_q=sum_{substack{h,s,t\(st,q)=1}}

u_h a_s b_t,e_q!left(A_qh(st)^{-1}ight). 	ag{1}
]
Suppose
[
 C^{-1}QHle STle CQH 	ag{2}
]
and, for some (	au>0),
[
 H^2Q^	aule Sle  rac{Q^{1-	au}}{H^2}. 	ag{3}
]
Then for every (epsilon>0),
[
 H^{-1}sum_{Qle q<2Q}|W_q|
 ll_{J,epsilon,	au,C}(QHST)^epsilon Q^{2-	au/4}. 	ag{4}
]
The same conclusion holds, with a fixed nonzero integer factor absorbed into the epsilon factor, for the fixed-target and (q)-dependent unit twists allowed by fact (73fb9efe1286f7e9); no coprimality condition on (h) is required.

06d784f21167c65a.proof:
Fact (73fb9efe1286f7e9) gives
[
 sum_{Qle q<2Q}|W_q|
 ll_{J,epsilon}(QHST)^epsilon Q(HT)^{1/2}
 left(HT+ rac{(HT)^2}{Q}ight)^{1/4}
 left(QS^2+S^4ight)^{1/4}. 	ag{5}
]
By (2),
[
 Tasymp_C  rac{QH}{S},qquad HTasymp_C rac{QH^2}{S}. 	ag{6}
]
Using ((X+Y)^{1/4}le X^{1/4}+Y^{1/4}) twice in (5), dividing by (H), and applying (6), the four resulting terms, apart from the common epsilon factor and constants depending on (C), are
[
 E_1=Q^2H^{1/2}S^{-1/4},qquad
 E_2=Q^{7/4}H^{1/2}S^{1/4}, 	ag{7}
]
[
 E_3=Q^2HS^{-1/2},qquad
 E_4=Q^{7/4}H. 	ag{8}
]
For completeness, these correspond respectively to choosing
[
 (HT)^{1/4}(QS^2)^{1/4},quad
 (HT)^{1/4}(S^4)^{1/4},quad
 ((HT)^2/Q)^{1/4}(QS^2)^{1/4},quad
 ((HT)^2/Q)^{1/4}(S^4)^{1/4}
]
from the two fourth roots in (5).

The lower inequality in (3) gives
[
 H^{1/2}S^{-1/4}le Q^{-	au/4},qquad
 HS^{-1/2}le Q^{-	au/2}. 	ag{9}
]
The upper inequality in (3) gives
[
 H^{1/2}S^{1/4}le Q^{(1-	au)/4}. 	ag{10}
]
Since a scale (S) satisfies both inequalities in (3), they also imply
[
 H^2Q^	aule  rac{Q^{1-	au}}{H^2},
 quadhbox{hence}quad
 Hle Q^{1/4-	au/2}. 	ag{11}
]
Consequently,
[
 E_1,E_2le Q^{2-	au/4},qquad
 E_3,E_4le Q^{2-	au/2}. 	ag{12}
]
Substitution into (5) proves (4). The final fixed-target and unit-twist assertion is inherited verbatim from the full estimate in fact (73fb9efe1286f7e9), which also explicitly requires no coprimality condition on (h).

0fd3b33d63081ee3.statement:
ONE-CAUCHY BOUND FOR A UNIQUE DILATOR STRATUM. Let \(\mathcal H\) be a complex Hilbert space with inner product linear in the first variable. Let \(X\) be a positive integer, let \(P\ge1\) be real, and let \(\mathcal P\) be a finite set of integers contained in \([P,2P]\). Let \(U_n\in\mathcal H\) for \(1\le n\le X\), with
\[
 \|U_n\|_{\mathcal H}\le B. \tag{1}
\]
For every \(a\in\mathcal P\) and \(m\le X/a\), let \(\lambda_{a,m}\) be complex, and suppose
\[
 \sum_{a\in\mathcal P}\sum_{m\le X/a}|\lambda_{a,m}|^2\le X. \tag{2}
\]
Put
\[
 S=\sum_{a\in\mathcal P}\sum_{m\le X/a}\lambda_{a,m}U_{am} \tag{3}
\]
and
\[
 \mathcal C_{\rm off}
 =\sum_{\substack{a,b\in\mathcal P\\a\ne b}}
 \left|
 \sum_{m\le X/\max(a,b)}
 \lambda_{a,m}\overline{\lambda_{b,m}}
 \langle U_{am},U_{bm}\rangle_{\mathcal H}
 \right|. \tag{4}
\]
Then
\[
 \|S\|_{\mathcal H}
 \le \left\lfloor\frac XP\right\rfloor^{1/2}
      \left(B^2X+\mathcal C_{\rm off}\right)^{1/2}. \tag{5}
\]
In particular, the diagonal contribution to (5) is at most
\[
 \frac{BX}{P^{1/2}}. \tag{6}
\]
Condition (2) holds whenever \(|\lambda_{a,m}|\le1\) and every integer \(n\le X\) has at most one representation \(n=am\) with \(\lambda_{a,m}\ne0\).

18c3a93006507edb.statement:
GA-GOODW-GENEALOGY: COMPLETE GOOD-W REFINEMENT RECOMBINATION AND MASK-FREE PRE-COPY COEFFICIENT. Fix tau>0 and the complete Good-W projection mathcal T_{good,sel}(tau) of fact e55b27d39c593164, namely the inherited beta>=6/5 post-low family restricted by the outer-scale condition mathscr W>=Q^tau. Fix the selected N, one active dyadic label, exact outer gcd g, orientation, normalized layer, actual-G layer, fixed-U/high layer, one allowed parent, and one smooth/projective physical source fibre. Before choosing a proper old refinement leaf, before the terminal marker, and before imposing any terminal-child-dependent dilator, hard, principal or centered projection, retain the complete family of auxiliary source, target-gcd, order/equality, collision, deletion and provenance siblings over this outer row.

Then their zero-one selectors satisfy the pointwise identity
[
 sum_{piin{cal P}_{good}}Pi_pi(q,h,n)=1                 	ag{1}
]
on every supported tuple (q,h,n). The same assertion holds for the complete family of canonical refinement descendants introduced inside each terminal anatomy: summing those descendants returns that anatomy coefficient.

Consequently, after all these auxiliary coordinates are recombined, the literal coefficient (9) of fact e55b27d39c593164 has the form
[
 c_lambda(q,h)=a_lambda(n)c_{base}(q,h;n),                  	ag{2}
]
where
[
 c_{base}(q,h;n)=
 mu(q){H_Pover n}widehat{mathcal F}_{q,n}
  (arepsilon_{or}h/n)
 e!left({arepsilon_{or}mathscr A hover qn}ight).       	ag{3}
]
Here every selected scale, beta, mathscr W, actual-G, fixed-U/high, parent, orientation and physical source-fibre restriction is already a fixed outer-row scalar or belongs to the literal smooth Fourier amplitude. The arithmetic gate (q,n)=1 and the frequency block F<=|h|<2F remain explicit in the row sum; they are not removed by (1).

Thus, after all marked-dilator blocks are reunited, the complete physical pre-copy row is exactly
[
 K_F^{agg}(n)=mu(n)
 sum_{substack{Qle q<2Q, Fle|h|<2F\(q,n)=1}}
 c_{base}(q,h;n)e_q(-mathscr A h n^{-1}),                    	ag{4}
]
and its squared modulus is the squared modulus of the same mask-free base row. Equations (1)-(4) are asserted only for the complete sibling union before a terminal-child-dependent projection. They do not permit removing a proper physical or refinement leaf, and they do not commute terminal aggregation through a d_lambda- or v_lambda-dependent hard, principal, centered or single-dilator projection. No bounded-variation estimate in n, no C-qSRC or N-qSRC supplier estimate, no F>Q principal estimate, no small-dilator estimate and no Goldbach conclusion is asserted.

1e357f0a9f079ca2.statement:
GA-GOODW-ONECOEFF-NREG: UNIFORM SCALED n-VARIATION OF ONE MASK-FREE GOOD-W FOURIER COEFFICIENT. Adopt fact 18c3a93006507edb and fix one of its complete Good-W outer rows, one integer q with Q<=q<2Q, and one h in a signed frequency block F<=|h|<2F. Exclude the explicit arithmetic gate (q,n)=1 and the terminal incidence. Extend n continuously as n=By on the fixed compact dyadic y-support and define
[
 C_{q,h}(y)=
 {H_Pover By}widehat{mathcal F}_{q,By}
   (arepsilon_{or}h/(By))
 e!left({arepsilon_{or}mathscr A hover qBy}ight).       	ag{1}
]
Then C_{q,h} is continuously differentiable. There is a fixed exponent C_0 such that, for every fixed A>0,
[
 {cal V}_y(C_{q,h})
 :=sup_y|C_{q,h}(y)|
   +int_{mathbb R}|partial_y C_{q,h}(y)|,dy
 ll_A(log x)^{C_0}(1+F/H_P)^{-A}.                            	ag{2}
]
The constant is uniform in the selected target, both orientations, every active Good-W outer row, q and h in the displayed ranges.

This is a one-coefficient analytic statement only. It does not sum (2) over the scalings on a primitive ray and does not assert a bounded-variation estimate for B_n^base(R) or F_{m,c}(n). The explicitly retained arithmetic terminal incidence, coprimality gates, and ray-pair/conductor partition are outside (2). No N-qSRC-SUP, C-qSRC-PV-BV-SUP, principal or small-dilator estimate, tail payment, or Goldbach conclusion is asserted.

24a573ab44a5fb68.statement:
GENERALIZED STOPPING IDENTITY AND FIVE-WAY ROUGH COVERAGE.

Let (2le Lle S<Z) be real numbers and let (r) be a squarefree positive integer satisfying
[
 r>S,qquad Z^4>r. 	ag{1}
]
Define
[
 v_L(r)=prod_{substack{pmid r\p {m prime}\ple L}}p,qquad
 omega_L(u)=#{p:pmid u, p {m prime}, ple L},
]
[
 I=(L,Z),qquad j_I(u)=#{p:pmid u, p {m prime}, L<p<Z}. 	ag{2}
]
For (kge2) and (yge1), define
[
 Gamma_{k;L,S}(y)=
 { f1}_{substack{y {m squarefree}\omega(y)=k\P^+(y)le L\S<y}}
  rac1k#{p:pmid y, p {m prime}, y/ple S}. 	ag{3}
]
Then exactly one of the following alternatives applies.

(A) (j_I(r)>0), and
[
 mu(r)=-sum_{substack{up=r\p {m prime}\L<p<Z}}
        rac{mu(u)}{1+j_I(u)}. 	ag{4}
]

(B) (j_I(r)=0) and (v_L(r)>S), and
[
 mu(r)=sum_{kge2}(-1)^k
 sum_{substack{uy=r\(u,y)=1}}
  rac{mu(u){ f1}_{j_I(u)=0}Gamma_{k;L,S}(y)}
      { inom{k+omega_L(u)}k}. 	ag{5}
]
Every nonzero marked (y) in (5) satisfies
[
 S<yle SL. 	ag{6}
]

(C) (j_I(r)=0) and (v=v_L(r)le S). Then (r=vw) uniquely, every prime divisor of (w) is at least (Z), and exactly one of
[
 w=p,qquad w=pq,qquad w=pqt 	ag{7}
]
holds, with distinct ordered primes (Zle p<q<t) in the applicable case. The exact signs are
[
 mu(vp)=-mu(v),qquad
 mu(vpq)=mu(v),qquad
 mu(vpqt)=-mu(v). 	ag{8}
]

Thus the five resulting classes—interval prime, stopping composite, and one, two, or three rough primes—cover every (r) in (1) exactly and preserve (mu(r)).

2775909b4db4ae24.statement:
DYADIC FIXED-POWER PAYMENT FOR HIGH-DENOMINATOR GENUINELY SEPARATED LONG TWO-ROW FIBRES.

Let Q tend to infinity, put \(B=Q^{4/3+o(1)}\), and fix \(3\le P\le Q\). Let \(\mathcal B\) be squarefree integers in \([B,2B)\), and let complex coefficients \(\gamma_b\) satisfy
\[
 \sum_{b\in\mathcal B}|\gamma_b|^2\le1. \tag{1}
\]
For every prime \(p\in[P,2P)\), let \(\mathcal B_1(p),\mathcal B_2(p)\subseteq\mathcal B\) consist of multiples of p. Assume that the sets \(\mathcal B_1(p)\) are disjoint as p varies and that every \(b\in\mathcal B\) belongs to at most \(Q^{o(1)}\) sets \(\mathcal B_2(p)\).

For every edge \(e=(p,b_1,b_2)\), where \(b_i\in\mathcal B_i(p)\), let \(\mathcal F_e\) be a finite set. For \(\nu\in\mathcal F_e\), choose positive squarefree integers \(d_{e,\nu,1},d_{e,\nu,2}\), both coprime to p, unit residues \(C_{e,\nu,i}\pmod {d_{e,\nu,i}}\), compactly supported infinitely differentiable weights \(W_{e,\nu,i}\), real centers \(X_{e,\nu,i}\), positive lengths \(L_{e,\nu,i}\), a nonzero slope \(c_{e,\nu}\pmod p\), and a complex coefficient \(\rho_{e,\nu}\). Assume all support, supremum, and derivative constants of the weights are \(Q^{o(1)}\), that \(d_{e,\nu,i}\le Q^C\) for one fixed C, and that
\[
 L_{e,\nu,i}\ge p d_{e,\nu,i}\quad(i=1,2), \tag{2}
\]
\[
 (d_{e,\nu,1}d_{e,\nu,2})^{1/2}\ge D, \tag{3}
\]
where D is independent of e and nu. Define
\[
 A_{e,\nu,f}=
 \sum_{\substack{x\in\mathbb Z\\(x,pd_{e,\nu,1})=1\\x\equiv f\ ({\rm mod}\ p)}}
 W_{e,\nu,1}\left({x-X_{e,\nu,1}\over L_{e,\nu,1}}\right)
 e_{d_{e,\nu,1}}(C_{e,\nu,1}x^{-1}), \tag{4}
\]
\[
 B_{e,\nu,g}=
 \sum_{\substack{y\in\mathbb Z\\(y,pd_{e,\nu,2})=1\\y\equiv g\ ({\rm mod}\ p)}}
 W_{e,\nu,2}\left({y-X_{e,\nu,2}\over L_{e,\nu,2}}\right)
 e_{d_{e,\nu,2}}(C_{e,\nu,2}y^{-1}), \tag{5}
\]
and
\[
 \mathcal T_{e,\nu}=
 \sum_{f,g\in\mathbb F_p^\times}
 {p\over p-1}\left({\bf1}_{g=c_{e,\nu}f}-{1\over p-1}\right)
 A_{e,\nu,f}B_{e,\nu,g}. \tag{6}
\]
Assume
\[
 \sum_{\nu\in\mathcal F_e}
 |\rho_{e,\nu}|L_{e,\nu,1}L_{e,\nu,2}
 \le Q^{2+o(1)} \tag{7}
\]
for every edge. Put
\[
 \mathcal T(P,D)=
 \sum_{\substack{P\le p<2P\\p\ {m prime}}}
 \sum_{b_i\in\mathcal B_i(p)}
 \overline{\gamma_{b_1}}\gamma_{b_2}
 \sum_{\nu\in\mathcal F_e}\rho_{e,\nu}\mathcal T_{e,\nu}. \tag{8}
\]
Then, for every \(\varepsilon>0\),
\[
 \boxed{|\mathcal T(P,D)|
 \ll_{C,\varepsilon}Q^{\varepsilon+o(1)}
 {BQ^2\over P^2D}.} \tag{9}
\]
In particular, if for some fixed \(\omega>0\)
\[
 D\ge {Q^{1+\omega}\over P^2}, \tag{10}
\]
then
\[
 |\mathcal T(P,D)|\ll Q^{7/3-\omega+\varepsilon+o(1)}. \tag{11}
\]

This theorem applies only after an exact decomposition into the genuinely separated smooth fibres (4)--(5) satisfying (7). It gives no bound for arbitrary common coefficients, consistently with fact f271bfac142ef5e0. It does not assert that the complete literal fibres of fact 8d3b2d43277f6d33 satisfy (2), (3), (7), or (10), and it gives no estimate for their complementary subfaces, the coefficient-one main faces, another incidence cell, or Goldbach.

29c43cef40f2e79b.statement:
EXACT VAUGHAN IDENTITY AND (P^{1/3})-TO-(P^{1/2}) BOX RANGES.

For arithmetic functions on the positive integers, let (*) denote Dirichlet convolution and let ({ f1}(n)=1). Fix (Uge1), and define
[
 mu_{le U}(n)=mu(n){ f1}_{nle U},quad
 mu_{>U}(n)=mu(n){ f1}_{n>U},
]
[
 Lambda_{le U}(n)=Lambda(n){ f1}_{nle U},quad
 Lambda_{>U}(n)=Lambda(n){ f1}_{n>U}. 	ag{1}
]
For every integer (n>U),
[
 Lambda(n)
 =(mu_{le U}*log)(n)
 -(mu_{le U}*Lambda_{le U}*{ f1})(n)
 +(mu_{>U}*Lambda_{>U}*{ f1})(n). 	ag{2}
]

Now let (P>8), put (U=P^{1/3}), and restrict (n) to
[
 Ple n<2P. 	ag{3}
]
After combining factors and subdividing into dyadic intervals, (2) is an exact finite sum of the following two kinds of boxes.

Type I: a factor (lle U) carries a coefficient bounded by a fixed divisor function times (log(2P)), and its complementary factor (m=n/l) is unweighted apart from a factor (log m) in the first term of (2).

Type II: two factors (l,mge U) satisfy (lm=n), at least one of them is strictly greater than (U), and both coefficients are bounded by a fixed divisor function times (log(2P)). After interchanging the factors so that (lle m), their dyadic scales satisfy
[
  rac12P^{1/3}le Lle(2P)^{1/2},qquad LMasymp P. 	ag{4}
]
Equivalently, up to absolute dyadic endpoint constants, the smaller-factor exponent lies between (1/3) and (1/2), with the lower endpoint included.

The identity and the range statement are algebraic. They do not assert an estimate for any Type-I or Type-II box.

29c43cef40f2e79b.proof:
The convolution identities
[
 Lambda=mu*log,qquad log=Lambda*{ f1},qquad
 mu*{ f1}=arepsilon 	ag{5}
]
hold, where (arepsilon(1)=1) and (arepsilon(n)=0) for (n>1). Split both (mu) and (Lambda) at (U). Then
[
  egin{aligned}
 Lambda
 &=mu_{le U}*log+mu_{>U}*log\
 &=mu_{le U}*log
   +mu_{>U}*Lambda_{le U}*{ f1}
   +mu_{>U}*Lambda_{>U}*{ f1}. 	ag{6}
 end{aligned}
]
Associativity, (5), and (mu_{>U}=mu-mu_{le U}) give
[
  egin{aligned}
 mu_{>U}*Lambda_{le U}*{ f1}
 &=(mu-mu_{le U})*{ f1}*Lambda_{le U}\
 &=Lambda_{le U}
   -mu_{le U}*Lambda_{le U}*{ f1}. 	ag{7}
 end{aligned}
]
At an integer (n>U), (Lambda_{le U}(n)=0). Substituting (7) into (6) proves (2).

We analyze its three terms under (3). The first term is
[
 sum_{substack{d k=n\dle U}}mu(d)log k, 	ag{8}
]
which is Type I.

The second term is
[
 -sum_{substack{d m k=n\dle U, mle U}}
 mu(d)Lambda(m). 	ag{9}
]
Combine (l=dm). Its coefficient
[
 a_l=sum_{substack{dm=l\dle U, mle U}}mu(d)Lambda(m) 	ag{10}
]
satisfies
[
 |a_l|le	au(l)log(2P), 	ag{11}
]
and (lle U^2). If (lle U), (9) is Type I. If (l>U), then
[
 k=n/lge P/U^2=P^{1/3}=U. 	ag{12}
]
Thus this is Type II under the corrected non-strict convention: (l>U) and (kge U), so at least one factor is strict.

The third term is
[
 sum_{substack{d m k=n\d>U, m>U}}
 mu(d)Lambda(m). 	ag{13}
]
Combine (t=mk). Then (d>U,t>U), and
[
 b_t=sum_{substack{mk=t\m>U}}Lambda(m) 	ag{14}
]
satisfies
[
 |b_t|le	au(t)log(2P). 	ag{15}
]
Thus (13) is Type II.

In every Type-II occurrence, interchange the factors if necessary and call the smaller one (l). Both factors are at least (U=P^{1/3}), while
[
 lle n^{1/2}<(2P)^{1/2}. 	ag{16}
]
A dyadic interval meeting (lge U) has lower endpoint at least (U/2), which gives (4). The product condition (lm=nin[P,2P)) gives (LMasymp P). Dyadic partitioning is exact after inserting any fixed smooth or sharp dyadic partition of unity, and creates only (O((log P)^2)) boxes. The coefficient bounds (11) and (15), together with (|mu|le1), prove the stated divisor-log bounds.

2d839168a73eedfa.statement:
Fix
\[
 \frac12<\alpha<\beta<1,\qquad \eta>0,\qquad\rho>0,\qquad
 B_{\rm src}>0.
\]
Let \(W,\Phi\) be continuously differentiable real functions with
bounded first derivatives, supported respectively in
\([\alpha,\beta]\) and \([1/2,2]\). Let \(N=2x\) be an integer with
\(x\ge3\), put \(T_x=(\log x)^{6+\eta+\rho}\), and assume
\[
 \frac12x^{1/2}(\log x)^{-B_{\rm src}}\le D\le2x^{1/2},
 \qquad D>2T_x,\qquad U>0.
\]
Define
\[
 {\cal I}_D=\{w\ge2:(w,N)=1,\ \mu(w)\ne0,\ w\le x^{1/2},\
                         \Phi(w/D)\ne0\},
\]
\[
 H_0=D(\log x)^{-6-\eta-\rho/2},\qquad
 {\cal H}_{\rm mid}=\{h\in\mathbb Z:
 H_0<|h|\le3D/2-H_0\}.
\]
For \(h\in{\cal H}_{\rm mid}\) and positive integers \(m,w,w-h\), put
\[
 P_1=N-mw,\qquad P_2=N-m(w-h),
\]
\[
\begin{split}
 \Omega(m,w,h)=&
 {\bf1}_{w,w-h\in{\cal I}_D}
 \Phi(w/D)\Phi((w-h)/D)W(mw/x)W(m(w-h)/x)\\
 &\times\Phi(P_1/U)\Phi(P_2/U),
\end{split}
\]
with value zero unless \(P_1,P_2>0\). Define
\[
 {\cal B}_{\rm mid}=
 \sum_{\substack{h\in{\cal H}_{\rm mid}\\m,w\ge1\\w-h\ge1}}
 \mu(w)\mu(w-h)\Omega(m,w,h)\Lambda(P_1)\Lambda(P_2).    \tag{1}
\]
Split it exactly as
\[
 {\cal B}_{\rm mid}={\cal B}_{\rm mid}^{\times}
                    +{\cal E}_{mN},                     \tag{2}
\]
where the first term is restricted to \((m,N)=1\) and the second to
\((m,N)>1\). Then, for every fixed \(\varepsilon>0\),
\[
 {\cal E}_{mN}
 \ll_{\varepsilon,W,\Phi}
 D x^{1/2+\varepsilon}(\log x)^2
 =o_A(xD(\log x)^{-A})                                  \tag{3}
\]
for every fixed \(A>0\).

Expand only the unit-\(m\) term in (2). For
\(h\in{\cal H}_{\rm mid}\) and positive integers
\(m,w,w-h,q_1,q_2\), define
\[
 A^\times_{h,q_1,q_2}(m,w)
 ={\bf1}_{(m,N)=1}\mu(w)\mu(w-h)\mu(q_1)\mu(q_2)
 \Omega(m,w,h)
 \log(P_1/q_1)_+\log(P_2/q_2)_+,                       \tag{4}
\]
and define it to be zero outside these positivity conditions. For every
nonzero divisor tuple in the expansion, with
\[
 d=(q_1,q_2),\qquad c=[q_1,q_2],\qquad b=q_2/d,
\]
one has
\[
 (N,c)=1,\qquad d\mid h.                                \tag{5}
\]
For each fixed \(h,q_1,q_2\), periodize (4) modulo \(c\) and put
\[
 \widehat A^\times_{h,q_1,q_2}(k,r)=
 \sum_{a_1,a_2\ ({\rm mod}\ c)}
 \sum_{\substack{m\equiv a_1\ ({\rm mod}\ c)\\
                 w\equiv a_2\ ({\rm mod}\ c)}}
 A^\times_{h,q_1,q_2}(m,w)e_c(ka_1+ra_2).              \tag{6}
\]
Define
\[
\begin{split}
 {\cal Z}_{\rm mid}^{\times}
 =\sum_{\substack{h\in{\cal H}_{\rm mid}\\q_1,q_2\ge1\\
                  (N,c)=1,\ d\mid h}}
 \frac1{c^2}\Bigg\{&
 \sum_{r\ ({\rm mod}\ c)}
 \widehat A^\times(0,r)e_b(-rhq_1^{-1})c_c(r)\\
 &+\sum_{k\ ({\rm mod}\ c)}
 \widehat A^\times(k,0)c_c(k)
 -\widehat A^\times(0,0)\phi(c)\Bigg\},                 \tag{7}
\end{split}
\]
and
\[
\begin{split}
 {\cal N}_{\rm mid}^{\times}
 =\sum_{\substack{h\in{\cal H}_{\rm mid}\\q_1,q_2\ge1\\
                  (N,c)=1,\ d\mid h}}
 \frac1{c^2}
 \sum_{\substack{k,r\ ({\rm mod}\ c)\\k\ne0,\ r\ne0}}
 \widehat A^\times(k,r)e_b(-rhq_1^{-1})S(r,kN;c).        \tag{8}
\end{split}
\]
The tuple subscripts in (7)--(8) are suppressed. Inactive nonsquarefree
moduli vanish; on active moduli the inverse in the twist is defined,
and the twist is one when \(b=1\).

Then
\[
 \boxed{
 {\cal B}_{\rm mid}
 ={\cal E}_{mN}+{\cal Z}_{\rm mid}^{\times}
                    +{\cal N}_{\rm mid}^{\times}
 }                                                       \tag{UNIT-M-PRECOMB}
\]
exactly, and
\[
