]
and L(1)=1. Hence R is supported on integers strictly larger than z.

Since ({ f1}*mu=delta_1), the definition of R gives
[
 M_z=mu*(delta_1-R).
]
As a formal identity of arithmetic functions,
[
 mu=M_z*(delta_1-R)^{-1}
     =M_z*sum_{kge0}R^{*k}.                                  	ag{8}
]
The coefficient of (R^{*K}) at an integer not exceeding (z^K) is zero, because every factor on the support of R is strictly larger than z. Thus (8) truncates after k=K-1 on that range. Expanding
[
 R=delta_1-{ f1}*M_z
]
and using the hockey-stick identity
[
 sum_{k=j-1}^{K-1}{kchoose j-1}={Kchoose j}
]
gives
[
 mu=sum_{j=1}^{K}(-1)^{j-1}{Kchoose j}
 M_z^{*j}*{ f1}^{*(j-1)}
]
at every integer at most z^K. This is (2).

Because Kc_0>beta,
[
 z^K=16^{-K}Q^{Kc_0}>2Q^ eta
]
for sufficiently large Q. Equation (1) then gives m<=n<=2Q^beta<z^K. If j=1 in (2), then m=d_1<=z<Q^a<Q^b, contradicting m>Q^b. Hence that sum is empty.

Now assume the representation is HB-nonsplittable. The empty submultiset has product one, so P exists. Condition (a) says that any subset product at most Q^b is smaller than Q^a; this proves (5).

Suppose some d_i is not chosen. If d_i=1, adjoining its occurrence leaves the numerical product equal to P and strictly enlarges the chosen submultiset, contrary to the inclusion-maximal tie-break. If d_i>1, then by the definitions of z and c_0,
[
 Pd_i<Q^a,Q^{c_0}/16le Q^b/16<Q^b.
]
Adjoining d_i gives a numerically larger admissible subset product, contradicting maximality. Thus every d_i is chosen.

Let y>1 be any omitted factor. If Py<=Q^b, adjoining y would give a numerically larger admissible subset product. Therefore Py>Q^b. Together with P<Q^a this gives
[
 y>Q^{b-a},
]
which proves (7), and omitted nonunit factors can only come from the unweighted family in (6).

It remains to prove that at least two such factors are omitted. If none were omitted, the chosen product would be n>Q^b, impossible. Suppose exactly one nonunit factor were omitted. Unit factors may all be included by the prescribed inclusion-maximal tie-break. If the sole omitted nonunit factor were t, all factors of m would be chosen, so P=m>Q^b, impossible. If it were u_r, all other nonunit factors would be chosen and hence P=n/u_r. Equation (5) would give n/u_r<Q^a, contradicting condition (b). Therefore at least two omitted factors are nonunit. This proves (6) and completes the proof.

