]
There are (j) such primes. Hence the right side of (5) equals
[
 -sum_{substack{pmid r\L<p<Z}} rac{mu(r/p)}j
 =- rac{j(-mu(r))}j=mu(r). 	ag{10}
]

Suppose next that (j_I(r)=0) and (v_L(r)>S). Randomly order the (n=omega_L(r)) prime divisors of (v_L(r)), and stop at the first prefix whose product exceeds (S). A stopping prefix exists. It has (kge2) primes, because each individual prime is at most (Lle S).

Fix a squarefree divisor (ymid v_L(r)) with (k) prime factors. The probability that its primes are exactly the first (k) revealed primes is ( inom nk^{-1}). Conditional on this event, each prime of (y) is equally likely to be last. The prefix first crosses (S) precisely when (y>S) and its last prime (p) satisfies (y/ple S). Therefore
[
 {mathbb P}(yhbox{ is the stopping prefix})
 = rac{Gamma_{k;L,S}(y)}{ inom nk}. 	ag{11}
]
Every ordering has exactly one stopping prefix, so
[
 sum_{kge2}sum_{substack{ymid v_L(r)\omega(y)=k}}
  rac{Gamma_{k;L,S}(y)}{ inom nk}=1. 	ag{12}
]
For such (y), set (u=r/y). Then
[
 (u,y)=1,quad n=k+omega_L(u),quad j_I(u)=0,quad
 (-1)^kmu(u)=mu(y)mu(u)=mu(r). 	ag{13}
]
Conversely, every nonzero term in (6) comes from one of these divisors. Equations (12)-(13) prove (6). If (p) is the last prime of an actual stopping prefix, then (y/ple S) and (ple L); consequently (S<yle SL), proving (7). Notice that this argument uses (Lle S), but nowhere compares (S) with (Z).

Finally suppose (j_I(r)=0) and (v=v_L(r)le S). Put (w=r/v). Since (r>Sge v), (w>1). Any prime divisor of (w) is not at most (L), by definition of (v), and is not in ((L,Z)), since (j_I(r)=0). It is therefore at least (Z), regardless of whether (S<Z) or (Sge Z). If squarefree (w) had at least four prime divisors, then (r=vwge wge Z^4), contradicting (2). It thus has exactly one, two, or three distinct prime divisors, giving (8). Multiplicativity of (mu) gives (9).

The alternatives are disjoint and exhaustive: first test whether (j_I(r)>0); if not, test whether (v_L(r)>S); in the remaining branch, the positive number of rough prime divisors is exactly one, two, or three. This proves the theorem.

