674a62f128f05a44.statement:
CHARACTER-DEPENDENT SLOPE DIAGONALIZATION WITH AN EXACT SMALL-COMMON-DIVISOR CUTOFF. Let p>2 be prime, let G>1 be real, and let \(\mathcal S,\mathcal Q\) be finite sets of positive integers coprime to p. For \(s\in\mathcal S\) and \(q\in\mathcal Q\), let \(X_s,Y_q:\mathbb F_p^\times\to\mathbb C\). For a multiplicative character \(\chi\) of \(\mathbb F_p^\times\), define
\[
 \widehat X_s(\chi)=\sum_{f\in\mathbb F_p^\times}X_s(f)\overline{\chi(f)},\qquad
 \widehat Y_q(\chi)=\sum_{g\in\mathbb F_p^\times}Y_q(g)\overline{\chi(g)}. \tag{1}
\]
For \(c\in\mathbb F_p^\times\), put
\[
 G_c(f,g)={p\over p-1}\left({\bf1}_{g=cf}-{1\over p-1}\right). \tag{2}
\]
For every nonprincipal \(\chi\), define
\[
 \lambda_c(\chi)=
 \sum_{\substack{s\in\mathcal S,\ q\in\mathcal Q\\
                   (s,q)<G,\ sq^{-1}\equiv c\ ({\rm mod}\ p)}}
 \widehat X_s(\overline\chi)\widehat Y_q(\chi),\qquad
 \Lambda_G(\chi)=\sum_{c\in\mathbb F_p^\times}\chi(c)\lambda_c(\chi), \tag{3}
\]
where \(q^{-1}\) is taken modulo p. Define
\[
 \kappa_G(h)=\sum_{\substack{d\mid h\\d<G}}\mu(h/d). \tag{4}
\]
Then
\[
 \mathcal K_G=
 \sum_{\substack{s\in\mathcal S,\ q\in\mathcal Q\\(s,q)<G}}
 \sum_{f,g\in\mathbb F_p^\times}
 G_{sq^{-1}}(f,g)X_s(f)Y_q(g) \tag{5}
\]
has the exact character decomposition
\[
 \boxed{\mathcal K_G={p\over(p-1)^2}
 \sum_{\chi\ne\chi_0}\Lambda_G(\chi)}, \tag{6}
\]
and every nonprincipal transform has the exact separated common-divisor formula
\[
 \boxed{\Lambda_G(\chi)=
 \sum_{h\ge1}\kappa_G(h)
 \left(\sum_{\substack{s\in\mathcal S\\h\mid s}}
       \chi(s)\widehat X_s(\overline\chi)\right)
 \left(\sum_{\substack{q\in\mathcal Q\\h\mid q}}
       \overline{\chi(q)}\widehat Y_q(\chi)\right).} \tag{7}
\]
Only finitely many h contribute. The slope weight in (3) is character-dependent because the row vectors are projected before they are aggregated over slopes. It reduces to the character-independent scalar slope weight of fact a3829b00a5a4b238 only under an additional common-fibre alignment. No estimate for (6) or (7) is asserted.

69e79ebdfba646b4.statement:
