In particular, the masks in (2) retain \(p,\ell\) as the canonical distinct primes, \(p\ell\asymp R\), the exact \(E_{11}\) prime-size and least-tag conditions, \((n,p\ell)=1\), \((p\ell,N)=1\), and \(0<|h|<Y_E\).

Let \(\mathscr A_E\) be the finite \(n\)-independent set of all quadruples
\(\alpha=(\iota,h,p,\ell)\) in the union of these literal ranges. In the common Hilbert space
\(\mathcal H_E=\ell^2(\mathscr A_E)\), with inner product linear in the first variable, define
\[
 V_n(\iota,h,p,\ell)=
 C_\iota(n;h,p,\ell)\,
 e\!\left(\frac{Nh}{np\ell}\right)
 \Pi_{>F_0}(n;h,p,\ell),                                                           \tag{3}
\]
again extending a missing coordinate by zero. Then:

(A) If \(T_{E11}^{>F_0}\) denotes the complete surviving primitive-conductor-\(>F_0\) part of the exact spectral reconstruction of \(T_{E11}^{\rm crit}\), with every literal copy and conjugate frequency retained, then
\[
 T_{E11}^{>F_0}
 =\sum_{n\ge1}\mu(n)\sum_{\alpha\in\mathscr A_E}V_n(\alpha)
 =\left\langle\sum_{n\ge1}\mu(n)V_n,\ {\bf1}_{\mathscr A_E}\right\rangle.          \tag{4}
\]
Thus all \(n\)-vectors occupy one common coordinate set; no \(n\)-dependent Hilbert-space identification is used.

(B) The auxiliary definition (1)--(3) is valid off the squarefree support. More precisely,
\[
 \mu(n)\mu(n)^2V_n=\mu(n)V_n                                                        \tag{5}
\]
for every \(n\). Hence a squarefree mask \(\mu(n)^2\) may be removed from the non-Mobius vector without changing (4). For every \(n\), character Parseval gives
\[
 |\Pi_{>F_0}(n;h,p,\ell)|\le \phi(Q_n)^{1/2}\le n^{1/2}.                           \tag{6}
\]
Consequently, for some fixed \(C_E>0\) depending only on the literal bounded smooth/copy inventory,
\[
 B_E:=\sup_{n\ge1}\|V_n\|_{\mathcal H_E}
 \ll x^{1/2}(\log x)^{C_E}.                                                        \tag{7}
\]
No multiplicativity of \(V_n\) is asserted or used.

(C) Let \(X_E\) be the largest integer \(n\) on which one of the literal \(n\)-masks in (2) is nonzero; thus \(X_E\ll D\), and put \(V_n=0\) for \(n>X_E\). Let \(\mathcal P\) be any finite nonempty set of primes, put
\[
 L_{\mathcal P}=\sum_{a\in\mathcal P}\frac1a,\quad
 P_-=\min\mathcal P,\quad
 \Omega_{\mathcal P}(n)=\sum_{a\in\mathcal P}{\bf1}_{a\mid n},
\]
and, for distinct \(a,b\in\mathcal P\), define the literal same-coordinate correlation
\[
 \mathfrak C_{a,b}=
 \sum_{m\le X_E/\max(a,b)}
 \langle V_{am},V_{bm}\rangle_{\mathcal H_E}.                                      \tag{8}
\]
Then the following finite Hilbert--Katai inequality holds:
\[
\begin{split}
 \left\|\sum_{n\le X_E}\mu(n)V_n\right\|_{\mathcal H_E}
 \le {}&
 \frac{B_E X_E^{1/2}}{L_{\mathcal P}}
 \left(\sum_{n\le X_E}
   (\Omega_{\mathcal P}(n)-L_{\mathcal P})^2\right)^{1/2}\\
 &+\frac{B_E}{L_{\mathcal P}}\sum_{a\in\mathcal P}
        \left\lfloor\frac{X_E}{a^2}\right\rfloor\\
 &+\frac{\lfloor X_E/P_-\rfloor^{1/2}}{L_{\mathcal P}}
 \left(B_E^2X_EL_{\mathcal P}
       +\sum_{\substack{a,b\in\mathcal P\\a\ne b}}
         |\mathfrak C_{a,b}|\right)^{1/2}.                                         \tag{9}
\end{split}
\]
The elementary variance in the first line satisfies
\[
 \sum_{n\le X_E}(\Omega_{\mathcal P}(n)-L_{\mathcal P})^2
 \le X_E L_{\mathcal P}
 +O\!\left(|\mathcal P|^2+L_{\mathcal P}|\mathcal P|\right).                      \tag{10}
\]
Thus, after the explicit variance, diagonal and \(a\mid m\) terms in (9), the smallest new arithmetic supplier is a bound for (8); no \(\mu^2(m)\) or coprimality selector occurs in (8).

(D) The exact coordinatewise phase in (8) is
\[
\begin{split}
 \langle V_{am},V_{bm}\rangle_{\mathcal H_E}
 =\sum_{\alpha=(\iota,h,p,\ell)\in\mathscr A_E}
 &C_\iota(am;h,p,\ell)\overline{C_\iota(bm;h,p,\ell)}\\
 {}\times&
 e\!\left(\frac{Nh}{mp\ell}\left(\frac1a-\frac1b\right)\right)
 \Pi_{>F_0}(am;h,p,\ell)
 \overline{\Pi_{>F_0}(bm;h,p,\ell)}.                                               \tag{11}
\end{split}
\]
Every source, frequency, prime, conductor, least-tag, smooth and marker mask therefore remains in the two-dilate correlation. If the projector in (1) is replaced by the complete character projector, the whole phase in (3) recombines to \(e_{p\ell}(Nh\,n^{-1})\), and the corresponding complete-projector phase in (11) is
\[
 e_{p\ell}\!\left(Nh\,m^{-1}(a^{-1}-b^{-1})\right),                               \tag{12}
\]
where the inverses exist because both literal masks impose \((am,p\ell)=(bm,p\ell)=1\). Formula (12) must not be substituted into (11) for the conductor-\(>F_0\) projector: the two truncated projectors in (11) are the exact remaining factors. Equations (4)--(12) give only an interface reduction. They do not estimate (8), do not by themselves pay the reconstruction norm \(\|{\bf1}_{\mathscr A_E}\|\) in (4), and make no assertion for \(E_{21}\), \(E_{22}\), PMD, logical capture, or Goldbach.

7264046d8776d59f.statement:
EXACT PHYSICAL-RESIDUE GRAM FOR THE CRITICAL TOTAL-MODULUS TMC BAND.

Assume all hypotheses and notation of fact \(ffa8f6d3f766b9f5\).  Fix
\(S>1\) with \((N,S)=1\) and \({\bf t}=(t_1,t_2)\in\mathbb R^2\).
For every tuple \(q_1,q_2,h\) occurring in equation (1) of that fact, put
\[
 d=(q_1,q_2),\qquad b=q_2/d.
\]
Let \(\gamma_{q_1,q_2,h}\pmod S\) be the unique residue satisfying
\[
 \gamma_{q_1,q_2,h}\equiv0\pmod {q_1},\qquad
 \gamma_{q_1,q_2,h}\equiv h\pmod {q_2}.                 \tag{1}
\]
It exists because \(d\mid h\).  Define
\[
 c_{q_1,q_2}({\bf t})
 =\mu(q_1)\mu(q_2)\widetilde F_{q_1}(t_1)
                         \widetilde F_{q_2}(t_2),        \tag{2}
\]
and, for \(z\pmod S\), define the literal physical residue row
\[
\begin{split}
 H_{S,{\bf t}}(z)=
 \sum_{\substack{q_1,q_2\ge1\\
                 \mu(q_1)\mu(q_2)\ne0\\
                 [q_1,q_2]=S}}
 c_{q_1,q_2}({\bf t})
 \sum_{\substack{h\in{\cal H}_{\rm mid}\\d\mid h}}
 \sum_{\substack{w\in\mathbb Z\\
        w\equiv\gamma_{q_1,q_2,h}-z\ ({\rm mod}\ S)}}
 g_{h,{\bf t}}(w).                                      \tag{3}
\end{split}
\]
Put
\[
 Z_{S,{\bf t}}(z)=S H_{S,{\bf t}}(z)-A_{S,{\bf t}}(0).  \tag{4}
\]
Then
\[
 A_{S,{\bf t}}(0)=\sum_{z\ ({\rm mod}\ S)}
 H_{S,{\bf t}}(z),                                      \tag{5}
\]
and the complete literal Kloosterman response of that fact has the exact
physical representation
\[
 \boxed{
 Y_{S,{\bf t}}(k)=
 \sum_{\substack{u\ ({\rm mod}\ S)\\(u,S)=1}}
 Z_{S,{\bf t}}(u)e_S(kNu^{-1}).
 }                                                       \tag{6}
\]

More generally, for any set \({\cal K}\) of residues modulo \(S\), define
\[
 {\cal D}_{\cal K}(a)=\sum_{k\in{\cal K}}e_S(ka).
\]
Then the exact band Gram is
\[
\boxed{
 \sum_{k\in{\cal K}}|Y_{S,{\bf t}}(k)|^2
 =
 \sum_{\substack{u,v\ ({\rm mod}\ S)\\(u,S)=(v,S)=1}}
 Z_{S,{\bf t}}(u)\overline{Z_{S,{\bf t}}(v)}
 {\cal D}_{\cal K}\!\left(N(u^{-1}-v^{-1})\right).
}                                                       \tag{7}
\]
In particular, deleting only \(k=0\) gives
\[
\boxed{
 \sum_{\substack{k\ ({\rm mod}\ S)\\k\ne0}}
 |Y_{S,{\bf t}}(k)|^2
 =
 S\sum_{\substack{u\ ({\rm mod}\ S)\\(u,S)=1}}
 |Z_{S,{\bf t}}(u)|^2
 -
 \left|\sum_{\substack{u\ ({\rm mod}\ S)\\(u,S)=1}}
 Z_{S,{\bf t}}(u)\right|^2.
}                                                       \tag{8}
\]
Consequently the energy in equation (4) of fact
\(ffa8f6d3f766b9f5\) is exactly
\[
 {\cal E}_S=\frac{M_0}{S^3}
 \left\{\int_{\mathbb R^2}
 \left(
 S\sum_{(u,S)=1}|Z_{S,{\bf t}}(u)|^2
 -\left|\sum_{(u,S)=1}Z_{S,{\bf t}}(u)\right|^2
 \right)^{1/2}d{\bf t}\right\}^2.                       \tag{9}
\]

For the critical centered band
\[
 {\cal K}_{\rm crit}
 =\{k\pmod S:0<\|k\|_S\le K_0\},\qquad K_0\asymp S/M_0,
\]
equation (7), with \({\cal K}={\cal K}_{\rm crit}\), is the literal
reflected/Möbius Gram that must be estimated before taking absolute values
over \(q_1,q_2,h\).  It retains all those signs inside \(H_{S,{\bf t}}\).
Equations (7)--(9) are identities and assert no logarithmic saving.
In particular, complete Parseval alone replaces the critical-band problem
by the nonnegative variance (8); any saving specific to the critical band
must control the signed off-diagonal Dirichlet-kernel form in (7), rather
than bound it by the complete energy or by a \(q_1,q_2,h\) triangle.

7fa6e823cd299d09.proof:
If \(n>C_0D\), all coefficients in (6) are zero by definition, so \(V_n=W_n=0\). We may therefore assume \(n\le C_0D\). If \(n\) is not squarefree, then \(\mu(n)^2=0\), so again \(W_n=0\). It remains to consider squarefree \(n\le C_0D\).

Every divisor of this \(n\), in particular \(Q_n=n/(n,h)\), is squarefree. If either indicator in (5) vanishes, then \(\Pi_{>F}=0\). Otherwise \(Q_n\ge2\), \((h_nr,Q_n)=1\), and the projector in (5) is exactly the projector \(\mathcal P_{q,a}^{>T}(t,u)\) of fact 9ef7fac8978ac244 with
\[
 q=Q_n,\qquad a=1,\qquad T=F,\qquad t=h_n,\qquad u=r.
\]
All hypotheses of that fact hold. Hence, for every \(\delta>0\),
\[
 |\Pi_{>F}(n;h,p,\ell)|
 \ll_\delta Q_n^\delta\ll_{\delta,C_0}x^\delta, \tag{8}
\]
because \(Q_n\le n\le C_0D\le C_0^2x\), the last inequality following from \(M\ge1\) and (1).

The phase in (6) has absolute value one. From (3) and (8), with \(\delta=\varepsilon/4\),
\[
 \begin{split}
 \|W_n\|_{\mathcal H}^2
 &\ll_{\varepsilon,C_0}x^{\varepsilon/2}
 \sum_{(p,\ell)\in\mathcal R}\sum_{h\ne0}
 \sum_{\iota\in\mathfrak L}|C_\iota(n;h,p,\ell)|^2\\
 &\ll_{\varepsilon,A,C_0}
 x^{\varepsilon/2}(\log x)^A
 \sum_{(p,\ell)\in\mathcal R}
 \left(\frac Mr\right)^2
 \sum_{h\ne0}\left(1+\frac{|h|M}{r}\right)^{-2J}. \tag{9}
 \end{split}
\]
For every \(y>0\), the function \(t\mapsto(1+t/y)^{-2J}\) is decreasing on the nonnegative real axis. Therefore
\[
 \begin{split}
 \sum_{h\ne0}(1+|h|/y)^{-2J}
 &=2\sum_{h\ge1}(1+h/y)^{-2J}\\
 &\le2\int_0^\infty(1+t/y)^{-2J}\,dt
 =\frac{2y}{2J-1}. \tag{10}
 \end{split}
\]
Apply this with \(y=r/M\). Using (2), each \(r\) is comparable to \(R\), and there are at most \(C_0R\) pairs. Therefore (9) and (10) give
\[
 \|W_n\|_{\mathcal H}^2
 \ll_{\varepsilon,A,C_0,J}
 x^{\varepsilon/2}(\log x)^A
 R\left(\frac MR\right)^2\frac RM
 \ll_{\varepsilon,A,C_0,J}
 Mx^{\varepsilon/2}(\log x)^A. \tag{11}
\]
For fixed \(A\), \((\log x)^A\ll_{\varepsilon,A}x^{\varepsilon/2}\). Taking square roots proves (7), after relabeling \(\varepsilon\).

831d32b948e6dcdb.statement:
ZERO-HEIGHT REPEATED-POLE OCCUPANCY AND ITS LITERAL SMALL-p NATURAL-COFACTOR ENVELOPE.

Let p be a prime, let S be a subset of F_p^* of cardinality L>=1, and let R_0 be a subset of F_p. For every r in R_0, h in F_p, and t in S, let A(r,h,t)>=0. Put
[
 U(r)=sum_{hin F_p^	imes}max_{tin S}A(r,h,t),qquad
 Z(r)=max_{tin S}A(r,0,t),
]
[
 E=sum_{rin R_0}U(r)^2,qquad Z_1=sum_{rin R_0}Z(r).                 	ag{1}
]
Give four positions the signs (+,+,-,-). For j=1,2, let (mathscr O_j) be any sub-sum of
[
 prod_{i=1}^4 A(r_i,h_i,t_i)                                   	ag{2}
]
over tuples having exactly j zero heights and for which
[
 mathcal R(X)=sum_{i=1}^4{sigma_i h_iover t_i(X+r_i)}
 in F_p(X)                                                       	ag{3}
]
is constant. An arbitrary tuple mask between zero and one may be inserted in (2). Then
[
  oxed{mathscr O_1le 4L^3Z_1E^{3/2}},qquad
  oxed{mathscr O_2le 6L^3Z_1^2E}.                              	ag{4}
]

Now use one fixed-k,U,Y,F recombined singleton Top-B fibre and one deterministic natural-cofactor block J_{p,k} of fact ddd3eb74fcd1957d, but replace its all-active restriction p>2F by
[
 ple 2F.                                                         	ag{5}
]
Let L=L_{p,k}<p, fix X in F_p, retain the same natural cofactor T=n/p and every literal source, selected-prime, pair-deletion and residual-CRT factor, and extend the definition of A_{p,k,X}(r,H,	au) to H=0 by the same source-copy l1 mass. For every epsilon>0, uniformly in all displayed residues,
[
 A_{p,k,X}(r,H,	au)
 ll_epsilon C_p,qquad
 C_p=Q^epsilon(1+Q/p)(1+F/p).                                   	ag{6}
]
If R_p is the number of shift residues represented by physical q in [Q,2Q), then
[
 R_plemin(p,Q+1),qquad
 Z_1ll R_pC_p,qquad Ell R_p(p-1)^2C_p^2.                       	ag{7}
]
Define
[
 mathscr B_{0,p,k,X}=L^4R_p^2(p-1)^4C_p^4.                       	ag{8}
]
Suppose a legal source-preserving common-X four-row formation has been made at the same selected prime p from two original ordered pairs, each satisfying the local nonresonance condition p
midOmega of fact 97b9d86408362df2. Then every zero-height exceptional tuple has either one or two zero local heights, and its total absolute occupancy (mathscr O_{m zero}) satisfies
[
  oxed{mathscr O_{m zero}
 ll {1over Lsqrt p},mathscr B_{0,p,k,X}.}                    	ag{9}
]
More explicitly, the one-zero and two-zero contributions are at most
[
 {8over Lsqrt p}mathscr B_{0,p,k,X},
 qquad {24over Lp}mathscr B_{0,p,k,X},                         	ag{10}
]
respectively. Hence if L>=p^delta, then
[
 mathscr O_{m zero}ll p^{-delta-1/2}mathscr B_{0,p,k,X}.    	ag{11}
]
If also p>=Q^zeta, this is a fixed fourth-moment saving and gives the factor
(Q^{-zeta(delta+1/2)/4}) after a fourth root.

This theorem pays the zero-local-height exceptional occupancy against the explicit coarse literal envelope (8) on long sub-p blocks in the small-p chamber. It does not construct the common-X operator, compare (8) with the complete upstream joint-completion Hilbert norm, pay short blocks, sum distinct selected primes or prove TOP-B-FIRSTP-JCOMP-ENV, TOP-B-BALG-DEEP-NR-SPEC, POST-A-TAIL, or Goldbach.

831d32b948e6dcdb.proof:
For each a in F_p, combine in (3) the terms with r_i=a. The coefficient of (X+a)^{-1} is
[
 c_a=sum_{i:r_i=a}sigma_i h_it_i^{-1}.
]
The functions (X+a)^{-1} for distinct a are linearly independent modulo constants: multiplying a relation by the product of the distinct X+a and evaluating at X=-a shows that each coefficient is zero. Moreover (3) tends to zero at infinity, so it is constant if and only if it is zero, equivalently
[
 sum_{i:r_i=a}sigma_i h_it_i^{-1}=0
 quadhbox{for every represented }a.                            	ag{12}
]

Assume exactly one height is zero. Every one of the other three coefficients (sigma_i h_it_i^{-1}) is nonzero. By (12), none of their shifts can occur alone. A partition of three active indices with no singleton has only one block, so all three active shifts are equal. Fix the zero position. Its total contribution is at most
[
 sum_{r_0,t_0}A(r_0,0,t_0)le LZ_1.                             	ag{13}
]
For the active triple at a fixed common shift r, fix their three nonzero heights and the first two cofactors. Equation (12) determines the third cofactor uniquely if it has a solution: the sum of the first two terms cannot be zero in a solution because the third term is nonzero. Therefore its weighted mass is at most
[
 L^2left(sum_{h
e0}max_tA(r,h,t)ight)^3=L^2U(r)^3.          	ag{14}
]
Summing r and using
[
 sum_rU(r)^3leleft(sum_rU(r)^2ight)^{3/2}=E^{3/2}
]
gives (L^3Z_1E^{3/2}) for each zero position. There are four positions, proving the first bound in (4). Any retained mask only decreases the sum.

Assume exactly two heights are zero. The two active coefficients are nonzero, so (12) forces their shifts to coincide. For fixed active heights and the first active cofactor, (12) determines the second active cofactor uniquely. Thus the active rows have total mass at most
[
 Lsum_rU(r)^2=LE.                                               	ag{15}
]
Each zero row costs at most LZ_1 independently. There are at most six choices of the two zero positions, proving the second bound in (4).

We prove (6). On a block of length L<p, a fixed cofactor residue (	au) determines at most one physical integer T. Then n=pT is fixed. The number of supported dyadic factorizations n=uy is divisor-bounded. A fixed shift residue r determines q modulo p and hence has at most (1+Q/p) physical lifts in [Q,2Q). The supported physical heights lie in the two signed intervals F<=|h|<2F. For any H in F_p, including H=0, the number of such heights congruent to H modulo p is (O(1+F/p)). Fact d63101722d12c88c bounds every recombined scalar coefficient by a divisor function, and fact 97b9d86408362df2 shows that the residual CRT factor has modulus one. Since every displayed scale is polynomial in Q, all divisor losses are (O_epsilon(Q^epsilon)). Multiplication proves (6).

There are at most p shift residues and at most Q+1 physical integers q in [Q,2Q), proving the first inequality in (7). Equation (6) gives (Z(r)ll C_p) and (U(r)ll(p-1)C_p). Summing over the represented shifts proves the remaining inequalities in (7).

Substitute (7) into (4):
[
 mathscr O_1ll
 4L^3R_p^{5/2}(p-1)^3C_p^4,
quad
 mathscr O_2ll
 6L^3R_p^3(p-1)^2C_p^4.                                        	ag{16}
]
After division by (8),
[
 {mathscr O_1overmathscr B_{0,p,k,X}}
 ll {4sqrt{R_p}over L(p-1)}
 le {8over Lsqrt p},
]
[
 {mathscr O_2overmathscr B_{0,p,k,X}}
 ll {6R_pover L(p-1)^2}
 le {24over Lp}.                                              	ag{17}
]
The elementary final inequalities hold for every prime p, including p=2. This proves (9)-(10).

For one original ordered pair, fact 97b9d86408362df2 gives p
midOmega. If both local heights of that pair were zero modulo p, the defining formula
[
 Omega=h_1q_2u_2-h_2q_1u_1
]
would give p|Omega, a contradiction. Hence each original pair contains at most one zero local height, so the four-row tuple contains at most two. The physical frequencies are nonzero integers, so there are no other height cases. Thus (9) covers every zero-height exceptional tuple in the stated same-p four-row formation.

Finally L>=p^delta inserted in (9) gives (11), and p>=Q^zeta gives the displayed fourth-root factor. The scope limitations remain because neither the common-X construction nor a comparison of the coarse envelope (8) with the upstream joint norm is among the hypotheses proved here.

875cb4f04516b35e.statement:
GA-TERMINAL-SQPROFILE: EXACT SUPPORTED A/C FORM OF THE SQUARED-MARKER DILATOR PROFILE AND MULTIPLICATIVE INTEGRAL WEIGHT. Fix the finite supported set mathcal N of squarefree integers from fact c384d36af25e96a6, with B/2<=n<=2B, and adopt its terminal marker with cutoff Z>2. For a squarefree integer d define
[
 r_Z(d)=#{p:pmid d, p {m prime}, 2<p<Z}.               	ag{1}
]
Let f be any complex sequence supported on mathcal N, let chi be a Dirichlet character modulo m, and restrict to terminal edges with D<=d_lambda<2D and (n,m)=1. Split the exact profile S_{chi,D}f of fact 256c10f372b85755 into its A and C parts.

For every integer d in [D,2D), the A part is exactly
[
 A^A_{chi,D}(d)=
 { f1}_{mu^2(d)=1}{1over(r_Z(d)+1)^2}
 sum_{substack{p {m prime}\2<p<Z, p
mid d\
                  dpinmathcal N\(dp,m)=1}}
 chi(p)f(dp).                                                   	ag{2}
]
The C part is exactly
[
 A^C_{chi,D}(d)=
 { f1}_{d>1}{ f1}_{mu^2(d)=1}{ f1}_{P^-(d)ge Z}
 sum_{substack{vin{1,2}\(d,v)=1\
                  dvinmathcal N\(dv,m)=1}}
 chi(v)f(dv),                                                   	ag{3}
]
where P^-(d) is the least prime factor for d>1. Thus S_{chi,D}f=A^A_{chi,D}+A^C_{chi,D}.

Moreover, for every nonnegative integer r,
[
 {1over(r+1)^2}=int_0^1 t^r(-log t),dt.                     	ag{4}
]
Hence the complete A-profile has the exact multiplicative-weight representation
[
 A^A_{chi,D}(d)=
 { f1}_{mu^2(d)=1}int_0^1
 t^{r_Z(d)}(-log t)
 sum_{substack{p {m prime}\2<p<Z, p
mid d\
                  dpinmathcal N\(dp,m)=1}}
 chi(p)f(dp),dt.                                              	ag{5}
]
Equations (2)-(5) apply to the supported full centered-source profile of fact bab94ab4a0e20bc0 by taking f(n)=F_{m,c}(n) on its inherited supported set mathcal N.

This theorem is an exact arithmetic normal form. It proves no cancellation for the weighted character sum over d. It does not remove the inherited support, squarefreeness, roughness, p not dividing d, coprimality, or the dependence f(dp), and invokes no sieve theorem. No N-qSRC-SUP, C-qSRC-PV-BV-SUP, principal or small-dilator estimate, tail payment, or Goldbach conclusion is asserted.

8d3b2d43277f6d33.statement:
EXACT FIRST LITERAL SMALL-CROSS-GCD SLOPE TRANSFORM WITH BOTH ADDITIVE TENSORS AND ITS REQUIRED SAVING.

Fix \(0<\eta<1/12\) and \(0<\delta<1\). Let Q tend to infinity and put
\[
 U=Q^{1/3},\qquad B=Q^{4/3+o(1)},\qquad F_0=Q^{1-2\eta+o(1)}. \tag{1}
\]
Let N be a fixed positive integer. Let \(w_n\) be supported on \(Q\le n<2Q\) and satisfy \(|w_n|\le Q^\varepsilon\) for every \(\varepsilon>0\). Define the two literal Vaughan families, extended by zero outside \(Q\le au<2Q\), by
\[
 \beta_{ss,a}(u)=\mu(a)w_{au}\sum_{\substack{m\mid u\\m\le U}}\mu(m)
 \quad(1\le a\le U), \tag{2}
\]
\[
 \beta_{ll,a}(u)=w_{au}
 \sum_{\substack{dm=u\\U<d\le m}}\varkappa(d,m)\mu(d)\mu(m)
 \quad(1\le a<2U), \tag{3}
\]
where \(\varkappa(d,m)=1\) if \(d=m\) and \(\varkappa(d,m)=2\) if \(d<m\). Put
\[
 \mathcal U_a=\{u\in\mathbb Z:Q\le au<2Q\}. \tag{4}
\]

Let \(\mathcal B\) be the squarefree integers in \([B,2B)\), and, for \(b\in\mathcal B\), put
\[
 L_b=\prod_{r\mid b,\ b/r<F_0}r. \tag{5}
\]
Let \(\mathcal R\) consist of b for which \(L_b>1\) and \((L_b,N)=1\). For \(b\in\mathcal R\), let \(\pi(b)\) be the largest prime divisor of \(L_b\) and put \(s(b)=b/\pi(b)\). Let complex numbers \(\gamma_b\) satisfy
\[
 \sum_{b\in\mathcal R}|\gamma_b|^2\le1. \tag{6}
\]

Fix \(1\le P\le Q\). For a prime \(p\in[P,2P)\), define
\[
 \mathcal S_p=\{s:ps\in\mathcal R,\ \pi(ps)=p\},\qquad
 \mathcal Q_p=\{q:pq\in\mathcal R,\ p\mid s(pq)\}. \tag{7}
\]
For \(s\in\mathcal S_p\), let \(M_1=M_1(p,s)\) satisfy
\[
 pM_1\equiv N\pmod s, \tag{8}
\]
with \(M_1=1\) if \(s=1\). For \(q\in\mathcal Q_p\), let \(M_{12}=M_{12}(p,q)\) satisfy
\[
 pM_{12}\equiv N\pmod q, \tag{9}
\]
with \(M_{12}=1\) if \(q=1\). Write
\[
 e_d(z)=\exp(2\pi i z/d),\qquad
 c_k(M)=\sum_{\substack{x\ ({\rm mod}\ k)\\(x,k)=1}}e_k(Mx). \tag{10}
\]
Define the following tensors by zero extension:
\[
 C_{p,s}(n)=0\quad\hbox{if }(n,ps)>1,\qquad
 J_{p,q}(n)=0\quad\hbox{if }(n,pq)>1. \tag{11}
\]
On their coprime supports define
\[
 \begin{split}
 C_{p,s}(n)=
 \sum_{\substack{h_1\mid s\\F_0/p<h_1\le s\\(h_1,M_1)=1}}
 {c_{s/h_1}(M_1)\over\phi(s/h_1)}
 \sum_{d_1\mid h_1}
 {e_{d_1}(M_1[n(s/d_1)]^{-1})\over\phi(h_1/d_1)}, \tag{12}\\
 J_{p,q}(n)=
 \sum_{\substack{h_2\mid q\\F_0/p<h_2\le F_0\\(h_2,M_{12})=1}}
 {c_{q/h_2}(M_{12})\over\phi(q/h_2)}
 \sum_{d_2\mid h_2}
 {e_{d_2}(M_{12}[n(q/d_2)]^{-1})\over\phi(h_2/d_2)}. \tag{13}
 \end{split}
\]
The inverses in (12)--(13) are taken modulo \(d_i\), and \(e_1=1\). Thus both parent labels \(h_i\), both additive denominators \(d_i\), their complementary cofactors, the Ramanujan/totient weights, the shifted/full boundary ranges, and the targets remain explicit.

For \(\tau\in\{ss,ll\}\), with a in the range stated in (2) or (3), and for \(f,g\in\mathbb F_p^\times\), define
\[
 X_{p,\tau,a,s}(f)=\overline{\gamma_{ps}}
 \sum_{\substack{u\in\mathcal U_a\\u\equiv f\ ({\rm mod}\ p)}}
 \beta_{\tau,a}(u)C_{p,s}(au), \tag{14}
\]
\[
 Y_{p,\tau,a,q}(g)=\gamma_{pq}
 \sum_{\substack{u\in\mathcal U_a\\u\equiv g\ ({\rm mod}\ p)}}
 \overline{\beta_{\tau,a}(u)}\,\overline{J_{p,q}(au)}. \tag{15}
\]
For a multiplicative character \(\chi\) of \(\mathbb F_p^\times\), use the transforms
\[
 \widehat X_{p,\tau,a,s}(\chi)=\sum_{f\ne0}X_{p,\tau,a,s}(f)\overline{\chi(f)},\quad
 \widehat Y_{p,\tau,a,q}(\chi)=\sum_{g\ne0}Y_{p,\tau,a,q}(g)\overline{\chi(g)}. \tag{16}
\]
Put
\[
 G_R=Q^{1+\delta}/P^2 \tag{17}
\]
and suppose \(G_R>1\). For every nonprincipal character chi modulo p define
\[
 \lambda_{p,\tau,a,c}(\chi)=
 \sum_{\substack{s\in\mathcal S_p,\ q\in\mathcal Q_p\\
                   (s,q)<G_R,\ sq^{-1}\equiv c\ ({\rm mod}\ p)}}
 \widehat X_{p,\tau,a,s}(\overline\chi)
 \widehat Y_{p,\tau,a,q}(\chi), \tag{18}
\]
and
\[
 \Lambda_{p,\tau,a}(\chi)=
 \sum_{c\in\mathbb F_p^\times}\chi(c)
 \lambda_{p,\tau,a,c}(\chi). \tag{19}
\]
If
\[
 \kappa_{G_R}(h)=\sum_{\substack{d\mid h\\d<G_R}}\mu(h/d), \tag{20}
\]
then exactly
\[
 \boxed{\Lambda_{p,\tau,a}(\chi)=
 \sum_{h\ge1}\kappa_{G_R}(h)
 \left(\sum_{\substack{s\in\mathcal S_p\\h\mid s}}
 \chi(s)\widehat X_{p,\tau,a,s}(\overline\chi)\right)
 \left(\sum_{\substack{q\in\mathcal Q_p\\h\mid q}}
 \overline{\chi(q)}\widehat Y_{p,\tau,a,q}(\chi)\right).} \tag{21}
\]
Only finitely many h contribute.

Define the centered small-cross-gcd boundary component
\[
 \begin{split}
 \mathcal K_{G,<}(P)=
 \sum_{\substack{P\le p<2P\\p\ {m prime}}}
 \sum_{\tau\in\{ss,ll\}}\sum_a
 \sum_{\substack{s\in\mathcal S_p,q\in\mathcal Q_p\\(s,q)<G_R}}
 \sum_{u_1,u_2\in\mathcal U_a}
 &\overline{\gamma_{ps}}\gamma_{pq}
 \beta_{\tau,a}(u_1)\overline{\beta_{\tau,a}(u_2)}
 C_{p,s}(au_1)\overline{J_{p,q}(au_2)}\\
 &\times {p\over p-1}
 \left({\bf1}_{u_1s\equiv u_2q\ ({\rm mod}\ p)}
             -{1\over p-1}\right), \tag{22}
 \end{split}
\]
where \(1\le a\le U\) for \(\tau=ss\) and \(1\le a<2U\) for \(\tau=ll\). Then
\[
 \boxed{\mathcal K_{G,<}(P)=
 \sum_{\substack{P\le p<2P\\p\ {m prime}}}
 \sum_{\tau}\sum_a{p\over(p-1)^2}
 \sum_{\chi\ne\chi_0}\Lambda_{p,\tau,a}(\chi).} \tag{23}
\]
The sum (22) is exactly the \(G_p(A,D)\overline{J_2}\) component of the literal \(K_{\rm inc}^{\rm rem}\) in fact b9560e346963b9e0 on the unproved range \((s_1,q_2)<G_R\), for the same sequence \(w_n\), Vaughan labels and a-ranges. Every displayed zero extension is precisely its inherited coprimality support.

Consequently the estimate
\[
 \sum_{\substack{P\le p<2P\\p\ {m prime}}}
 \sum_\tau\sum_a{p\over(p-1)^2}
 \sum_{\chi\ne\chi_0}|\Lambda_{p,\tau,a}(\chi)|
 \ll Q^{7/3-\omega} \tag{24}
\]
for some fixed \(\omega>0\) is sufficient to pay this component. Fact 39071136bb037885 pays every complementary remainder pair with
\[
 (s_1,q_2)\ge\max\{1,Q^{1+\delta}/P^2\}. \tag{25}
\]
In the power-deficient range \(P^2\le Q\), its complete-remainder absolute envelope at \(G=1\) is
\[
 Q^{o(1)}B\left({Q^2\over P^2}+{Q\log Q\over P}\right)
 =Q^{o(1)}{Q^{10/3}\over P^2}. \tag{26}
\]
Thus (24) requires, relative to (26), the saving factor \(Q^{1+\omega-o(1)}/P^2\), or equivalently a retained fraction at most
\[
 {P^2\over Q}Q^{-\omega+o(1)}. \tag{27}
\]

The weight (18) is character-dependent because both complete row tensors are projected before slope aggregation. It is not the scalar \(\lambda_c\) of fact a3829b00a5a4b238 unless an additional common-fibre alignment is proved. Fact 6ab529accdede090 applies to an individual two-row fibre only if both row weights are genuinely separated and smooth. The product-coupled literal coefficients in (14)--(15) have no such automatic denominator gain, by fact f271bfac142ef5e0. No estimate (24), no bound for the coefficient-one main faces or the other remainder faces, no complete small-cross-gcd payment, and no Goldbach conclusion is asserted.

90ee649f2811580d.statement:
CENTERED DILATOR-INVERSE SQUARE FORM OF THE CROSSED PHYSICAL FRAME. Let \(N,m\) be positive integers and \(\mathcal P\) a finite set of integers. Let \(\mathscr A\) be a finite set of coordinates
\[
 \alpha=(\iota,h,p,\ell),\qquad h\ne0,
\]
where \(p,\ell\) are distinct primes, and put \(u_\alpha=p\ell\), \(\gamma(\alpha)=(h,u_\alpha)\). Assume \((Nmh,u_\alpha)=1\). Let \(E_\alpha>0\). For \(a\in\mathcal P\), let \(C_a(\alpha)\) be complex and extend it by zero unless \((a,u_\alpha)=1\).

For an ordered pair \((\alpha_1,\alpha_2)\) with \(\gamma(\alpha_1)\ne\gamma(\alpha_2)\), write \(\gamma(\alpha_i)=(h_i,u_i)\), and define
\[
 L_{12}={\rm lcm}(u_1,u_2),qquad
 \mathcal P_{12}=\{a\in\mathcal P:(a,L_{12})=1\},                    \tag{1}
\]
\[
 A_{12,m}\equiv Nm^{-1}(L_{12}h_1/u_1-L_{12}h_2/u_2)
 \pmod{L_{12}},                                                         \tag{2}
\]
\[
 Z_{a,m}(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)
 =\mu(m)^2\frac{C_a(\alpha_1)\overline{C_a(\alpha_2)}}
 {\sqrt{E_{\alpha_1}E_{\alpha_2}}}.                                  \tag{3}
\]
Then \(Z_{a,m}(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)=0\) for every \(a\notin\mathcal P_{12}\). Define
\[
\begin{split}
 \mathfrak O_m^{\rm coord}
 =\sum_{\substack{\alpha_1,\alpha_2\in\mathscr A\\
 \gamma(\alpha_1)\ne\gamma(\alpha_2)}}
 \sum_{\substack{a,b\in\mathcal P_{12}\\a\ne b}}
 &Z_{a,m}(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)
 \overline{Z_{b,m}(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)}\\
 &\times e_{L_{12}}\!\left(A_{12,m}(a^{-1}-b^{-1})\right).
                                                                         \tag{4}
\end{split}
\]
Then
\[
\boxed{
 \mathfrak O_m^{\rm coord}
 =\sum_{\substack{\alpha_1,\alpha_2\in\mathscr A\\
 \gamma(\alpha_1)\ne\gamma(\alpha_2)}}
 \left[
 \left|\sum_{a\in\mathcal P_{12}}
 Z_{a,m}(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)e_{L_{12}}(A_{12,m}a^{-1})\right|^2
 -\sum_{a\in\mathcal P_{12}}|Z_{a,m}(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)|^2
 \right].
}                                                                        \tag{5}
\]
For the literal data of fact 754cf92eea3a7532, retain its coordinates \(\alpha=(\iota,h,p,\ell)\) without relabeling and take \(C_a(\alpha)=C_a(m;\alpha)\). Then
\[
 \mathfrak O_m^{\rm coord}=\mathfrak O_m.
\]
Thus (5) is the exact jointly centered inverse-frame form of its OFF-FRAME supplier. Replacing the bracket by its positive square is not exact. No bound for (5), E11 closure, or Goldbach conclusion is asserted.

9ef7fac8978ac244.statement:
DIVISOR BOUND FOR A SQUAREFREE COMMON-CORE CONDUCTOR PROJECTOR. Let \(N\) be a positive integer, let \(q\ge2\) be squarefree, let \(a,t,u\) be integers satisfying
\[
 (a,qtu)=1,\qquad (q,tu)=1,
\]
and let \(T\ge1\) be real. Put \(e_q(y)=\exp(2\pi i y/q)\). For a Dirichlet character \(\psi\) modulo \(q\), let \(f_\psi={\rm cond}(\psi)\) be its primitive conductor and define
\[
 G_{N,q}(\psi)=\sum_{\substack{z\ ({\rm mod}\ q)\\(z,q)=1}}
 e_q(-Nz)\overline{\psi(z)}
\]
and
\[
 \mathcal P_{q,a}^{>T}(t,u)=\frac1{\phi(q)}
 \sum_{\substack{\psi\ ({\rm mod}\ q)\\f_\psi>T\\(f_\psi,N)=1}}
 G_{N,q}(\psi)\psi(t)\overline{\psi(au)}. \tag{1}
\]
Then
\[
 \left|\mathcal P_{q,a}^{>T}(t,u)\right|
 \le \tau(q)\frac{q}{\phi(q)}
 \le \tau(q)^2. \tag{2}
\]
Consequently, for every real \(\varepsilon>0\),
\[
 \left|\mathcal P_{q,a}^{>T}(t,u)\right|\ll_\varepsilon q^\varepsilon, \tag{3}
\]
uniformly in \(N,a,t,u,T\) subject to the displayed hypotheses. Here \(\tau(q)\) is the number of positive divisors of \(q\).

b02d45f4a07a5524.statement:
Let q>1 be an integer and let μ be the Möbius function. Let J be a finite set, let z_j∈C and x_j∈Z for j∈J, and, for every divisor e of q, define F_e(v)=∑_{j∈J} z_j exp(2πivx_j/e) for v modulo e. Define C(e)=∑_{v (mod e)}|F_e(v)|^4, S=∑_{j∈J}z_j, and, for every divisor r>1 of q, P(r)=∑_{a (mod r),(a,r)=1}|F_r(a)|^4. Then
(1) ∑_{u (mod q),(u,q)=1}|F_q(u)|^4 = ∑_{e|q} μ(q/e)C(e);
(2) writing C(e)=|S|^4+\widetilde C(e), one has \widetilde C(e)=∑_{r|e,r>1}P(r) and hence ∑_{e|q}μ(q/e)\widetilde C(e)=P(q);
(3) the common zero-mode contribution cancels because ∑_{e|q}μ(q/e)=0. In particular, if S≠0, then ∑_{e|q}C(e)≥τ(q)|S|^4 whereas the contribution of |S|^4 to the exact signed sum in (1) is zero. Consequently, the raw moments C(e) overlap in their common zero-mode term and their unsigned sum repeats that term τ(q) times. Centering each C(e) and then applying the signed Möbius recombination in (2) is an exact way to account for this overlap without retaining any proper reduced-conductor contribution.

