eeb2a1f90b2a4cb2.statement:
SQUAREFREE-ZERO HILBERT--KATAI ALTERNATIVE AND THE EXACT SELECTOR TRADEOFF. Let \(\mathcal H\) be a complex Hilbert space whose inner product is linear in the first variable. Let \(X\) be a positive integer and let \(V_n\in\mathcal H\) for \(1\le n\le X\), with
\[
 \|V_n\|_{\mathcal H}\le B.
\]
Put \(V_n=0\) for \(n>X\), let \(\mu\) be the Möbius function, and define
\[
 W_n=\mu(n)^2V_n. \tag{1}
\]
Let \(\mathcal P\) be a finite nonempty set of primes, and put
\[
 L_{\mathcal P}=\sum_{a\in\mathcal P}\frac1a,\qquad
 P_-=\min\mathcal P,\qquad
 \Omega_{\mathcal P}(n)=\sum_{a\in\mathcal P}{\bf1}_{a\mid n}. \tag{2}
\]
For distinct \(a,b\in\mathcal P\), define
\[
 \mathfrak C^{\rm sq}_{a,b}
 =\sum_{m\le X/\max(a,b)}
   \mu(m)^2{\bf1}_{(ab,m)=1}
   \langle V_{am},V_{bm}\rangle_{\mathcal H}. \tag{3}
\]
Then
\[
 \sum_{n\le X}\mu(n)W_n=\sum_{n\le X}\mu(n)V_n, \tag{4}
\]
and the finite Hilbert--Katai inequality
\[
\begin{split}
 \left\|\sum_{n\le X}\mu(n)V_n\right\|_{\mathcal H}
 \le {}&
 \frac{BX^{1/2}}{L_{\mathcal P}}
 \left(\sum_{n\le X}
  (\Omega_{\mathcal P}(n)-L_{\mathcal P})^2\right)^{1/2}\\
 &+\frac{\lfloor X/P_-\rfloor^{1/2}}{L_{\mathcal P}}
 \left(
 B^2XL_{\mathcal P}
 +\sum_{\substack{a,b\in\mathcal P\\a\ne b}}
  |\mathfrak C^{\rm sq}_{a,b}|
 \right)^{1/2} \tag{5}
\end{split}
\]
holds. The elementary variance obeys
\[
 \sum_{n\le X}(\Omega_{\mathcal P}(n)-L_{\mathcal P})^2
 \le XL_{\mathcal P}
 +O\!\left(|\mathcal P|^2+
 L_{\mathcal P}|\mathcal P|\right). \tag{6}
\]

Thus choosing the squarefree-zero extension removes the separate
\[
 \frac{B}{L_{\mathcal P}}
 \sum_{a\in\mathcal P}\left\lfloor\frac X{a^2}\right\rfloor
\]
term that occurs when arbitrary nonzero off-squarefree vector values are retained. The exact price is the squarefree and coprimality selector in (3). No multiplicativity of \(V_n\) or \(W_n\) is assumed.

eed7c65b2f8b19a3.statement:
Under the hypotheses and notation of fact 3bb378b0a49679f9, the four possible exact gcd layers and their quotient-modulus primitive additive conductors are:
(1) if g_z=1, then b_z=p ell_p and e can be 1,p,ell_p,p ell_p;
(2) if g_z=p, then b_z=ell_p and e can be 1,ell_p;
(3) if g_z=ell_p, then b_z=p and e can be 1,p;
(4) if g_z=p ell_p, then b_z=1 and e=1 is the only additive conductor.
In the fourth layer the normalized fixed-N equation is a_zm+k=n_z and the quotient-modulus congruence is automatic, so its additive detector has no nontrivial unit-frequency or primitive-conductor part.

Consequently, within the specific component reconstruction architecture of facts f5b31dd3643732ac and 4a9a213a428b7e8c, if one requires the primitive conductor q_z of a leaf to be the physical quotient-modulus conductor e produced by fact 3bb378b0a49679f9, then no q_z>1 leaf arises from the maximal-gcd layer g_z=p ell_p. A complete formula in that architecture must therefore either place this layer in the real remainder R_{F,11}, prove that it is absent, or supply a different explicitly defined nontrivial physical modulus. No claim is made that the maximal-gcd layer is nonempty or large, or that every possible transform must use q_z=e.

